Greedy A-optimal selection beats random selection on all three pools, by 6--11\,\% of the random error, and several heuristics do no better than random. Top: gap to random in standardised RMSE (selector minus random, grid-averaged over $k=1..\min(10,N/2)$; negative is better; bars: 95\,\% family-clustered bootstrap CI of the paired difference); the dashed line is the pre-registered margin of 10\,\% of the random error, which only MTEB-Multi meets. The oracle (best of sampled sets, chosen on the test columns) is a test-leaking bound. Bottom: the primary gap at each $k$; it shrinks as $k$ grows and random sets catch up.